{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "It is true that a stretch of DNA that increases the risk of severe illness from COVID-19 was passed down from Neanderthals approximately 60,000 years ago. Scientific research has identified a specific segment of the human genome on chromosome 3 that is associated with a higher risk of severe COVID-19, and genetic analysis has shown that this segment was inherited from Neanderthals through interbreeding with modern humans tens of thousands of years ago. This Neanderthal-derived genetic variant is present in a significant portion of the population today and has been linked to increased susceptibility to severe outcomes from COVID-19 infection [1][2]."
}

