  "explanation": "There is evidence suggesting that ultraviolet (UV) light is associated with lower COVID-19 growth rates. Studies have found that higher levels of UV radiation are correlated with reduced transmission of COVID-19, likely due to the virus's decreased stability and viability when exposed to UV light. This relationship has been observed in epidemiological analyses that show regions with greater UV exposure tend to experience slower COVID-19 growth rates, supporting the idea that UV light can play a role in mitigating the spread of the virus [1][2]."
}

