}

{
  "answer": "yes",
  "explanation": "It is true that health care workers on the frontlines of the coronavirus pandemic suffer elevated rates of mental health issues. Multiple studies and reports have documented increased levels of anxiety, depression, insomnia, and post-traumatic stress symptoms among frontline health care workers during the COVID-19 pandemic. These mental health challenges are attributed to factors such as high workload, risk of infection, fear of transmitting the virus to family members, and witnessing high rates of patient morbidity and mortality [1][2]. The psychological impact on health care workers has been recognized as a significant concern, prompting calls for increased mental health support and interventions for this population [1][2]."
}

