  "explanation": "Individuals who are immune to the COVID-19 virus, either through previous infection or vaccination, are less likely to contract and transmit the virus to others. This reduction in the number of susceptible hosts helps to slow the spread of the virus within the community, thereby contributing to a decrease in the overall length and severity of the outbreak. As more people become immune, the virus has fewer opportunities to propagate, which can lead to the eventual end of the outbreak through herd immunity. This concept is supported by epidemiological principles and is a key reason why vaccination campaigns are critical in controlling infectious disease outbreaks."
}

